<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  dev flavour only. Replaces the strict file in the main source set.

  Two exceptions live here, for two different servers, and they are deliberately not the same
  exception.

  CLEARTEXT, for Keycloak. The local test stack runs `start-dev` on plain HTTP. The emulator reaches
  it over `adb reverse tcp:18080 tcp:18080`, i.e. through the device's own loopback, so cleartext is
  permitted to localhost and to 10.0.2.2 (the documented host route, kept for a stack published
  without adb reverse) and to nothing else. The base config stays closed, so a dev build talking to
  any other server still refuses cleartext.

  A TRUST ANCHOR, for the backend. The backend serves HTTPS with a certificate the public CAs know
  nothing about, so a dev build's API calls fail TLS validation before carrying a single byte — and
  the failure surfaces as `ApiError.Network`, i.e. as "you are offline", which is the least
  informative thing it could say.

  The anchor is the test stack's SHARED certificate, committed at `docker/test-tls/` in the main
  repository and bundled here. An earlier revision used the user certificate store instead, on the
  grounds that the test stack's keystore was generated locally per developer and a bundled
  certificate would be one person's, work for nobody else, and rot the first time anyone regenerated
  theirs. That reasoning was sound and its premise is gone: the test stack now ships one keystore for
  everybody, so the certificate below is everybody's and cannot rot. What it buys is that a developer
  — and CI — has nothing to install: the emulator's certificate-install flow cannot be driven by adb
  on a Play-Store system image, which made the old step a manual gate on every new AVD. See ADR-0139.

  `src="user"` stays alongside it, so a proxy CA someone installs by hand for debugging still works.
  The system anchors stay too: debug-overrides ADD to the other configurations rather than replacing
  them, and listing only these two would leave a build that trusts the test stack and nothing else.

  The bundled certificate is a trust anchor, not a secret, and the private key that matches it can
  only serve the loopback / emulator / docker-network names in its SAN list. The CA key that signed
  it was destroyed at generation time and exists nowhere, so nothing can mint a further certificate
  this build would trust.

  Two independent reasons a release build cannot inherit any of this:
    1. This file exists only in the dev source set; the prod flavour has no override.
    2. `<debug-overrides>` is honoured only when `android:debuggable="true"`. Even pasted into the
       main source set, the trust anchors below would be ignored by a release APK.
  The first is the intent; the second is the backstop for when someone edits the wrong file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="@raw/basetool_test_ca" />
            <certificates src="user" />
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
